---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import GuideDisclaimer from '../components/guide/GuideDisclaimer.astro';
import GuideVisual from '../components/guide/GuideVisual.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Find Your Starting Point — Return"
  description="A beginner-friendly guide to identify your current nervous system capacity and find your entry point to coherence practice."
  lang="en"
>
  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-6 lg:px-8 pt-32 pb-24">
    <p class="micro-label-accent mb-4">
      <a href="/#protocol" class="hover:text-sapphire-300 transition-colors">Protocol</a>
    </p>
    <h1 class="text-4xl sm:text-5xl font-light tracking-tight text-white leading-tight mb-6">
      Find Your Starting Point
    </h1>
    <p class="text-[17px] text-gray-400 font-light leading-relaxed mb-10">
      A compassionate, beginner-friendly guide to help you identify your current nervous system capacity and find an entry point to coherence practice. This is for people who may have tried meditation and found it frustrating, who think they “can’t do stillness,” or who feel broken or defective. You are not.
    </p>

    <GuideVisual kind="ripple" />

    <section class="mb-16">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-light tracking-tight text-white mb-5">A Simple Question</h2>
      <p class="text-[17px] text-gray-300 font-light leading-relaxed mb-4">
        Set a timer for 60 seconds. Sit quietly. Do nothing. Just notice what happens inside you.
      </p>
      <p class="text-[17px] text-gray-300 font-light leading-relaxed mb-6">Which best describes your experience?</p>
      <div class="space-y-3 mb-6">
        <div class="rounded-md border border-gray-800 bg-black/30 p-5">
          <p class="micro-label mb-2">Option 1</p>
          <p class="text-[15px] text-gray-300 font-light leading-relaxed">That was easy, pleasant. I settled naturally.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="rounded-md border border-gray-800 bg-black/30 p-5">
          <p class="micro-label mb-2">Option 2</p>
          <p class="text-[15px] text-gray-300 font-light leading-relaxed">I could do it, but there was restlessness or discomfort.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="rounded-md border border-gray-800 bg-black/30 p-5">
          <p class="micro-label mb-2">Option 3</p>
          <p class="text-[15px] text-gray-300 font-light leading-relaxed">I couldn’t really do it. My mind pulled elsewhere or my body wanted to move.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="text-[16px] text-gray-400 font-light leading-relaxed">
        This is not a test. There is no right answer. It’s just information about where your nervous system currently lives.
      </p>
    </section>

    <GuideVisual kind="paths" />

    <section class="mb-16">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-light tracking-tight text-white mb-5">Three Paths, Same Destination</h2>
      <div class="space-y-8">
        <div>
          <p class="micro-label-accent mb-3">Path 1 · Option 1</p>
          <p class="text-[17px] text-gray-300 font-light leading-relaxed">
            Stillness is accessible. You can work with the pause directly. The practices will feel natural. Your nervous system already knows stillness is safe.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p class="micro-label-accent mb-3">Path 2 · Option 2</p>
          <p class="text-[17px] text-gray-300 font-light leading-relaxed">
            Stillness is a negotiation. You’re in conversation with it. The restlessness itself is valuable—it’s showing you where your system hasn’t yet settled. That awareness is already the beginning of coherence. Stay with the practice. The restlessness will gradually soften.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p class="micro-label-accent mb-3">Path 3 · Option 3</p>
          <p class="text-[17px] text-gray-300 font-light leading-relaxed">
            Stillness doesn’t feel safe yet. Your nervous system learned early that absence of stimulation meant danger. You’re not broken. Your system is doing exactly what it was trained to do. For you, start with the wave, not the pause. Focus on continuous rhythm (inhale 4–5 sec, exhale 4–5 sec). Don’t force stillness. Let it arrive when your system is ready—typically 2–4 weeks.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <GuideVisual kind="breathline" />

    <section class="mb-16">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-light tracking-tight text-white mb-5">What is the Breathline?</h2>
      <p class="text-[17px] text-gray-300 font-light leading-relaxed mb-4">
        The breathline is a threshold. It’s where coherence becomes operational.
      </p>
      <p class="text-[17px] text-gray-300 font-light leading-relaxed mb-4">
        Every breath has two moments where you cross from motion into stillness:
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2 text-[17px] text-gray-300 font-light leading-relaxed mb-4">
        <li>The pause at the top of the inhale</li>
        <li>The pause at the bottom of the exhale</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="text-[17px] text-gray-300 font-light leading-relaxed">
        These pauses are the breathline. They’re the transition points where your system shifts from fluctuation into coherence. This is where resolution happens—patterns become visible, decisions clarify, emotions settle.
      </p>
    </section>

    <GuideVisual kind="practice" />

    <section class="mb-16">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-light tracking-tight text-white mb-5">How to Practice the Breathline</h2>

      <h3 class="text-lg font-medium text-white mt-8 mb-3">For Path 1</h3>
      <p class="text-[17px] text-gray-300 font-light leading-relaxed mb-4">
        Select the 4-4-4-4 pattern in the app or create your own, e.g. 4-2-4-2.
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2 text-[17px] text-gray-300 font-light leading-relaxed mb-4">
        <li>Inhale slowly for 4 counts</li>
        <li>Pause at the top (2–4 counts) — this is the first breathline</li>
        <li>Exhale slowly for 4 counts</li>
        <li>Pause at the bottom (2–4 counts) — this is the second breathline</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="text-[17px] text-gray-300 font-light leading-relaxed">Repeat for 9 cycles and gradually extend.</p>

      <h3 class="text-lg font-medium text-white mt-8 mb-3">For Path 2</h3>
      <p class="text-[17px] text-gray-300 font-light leading-relaxed">
        Same practice, but when restlessness arises at the pause, notice it. Don’t resist it. What does it feel like? Thought? Physical sensation? The restlessness is information. Stay with the practice.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-lg font-medium text-white mt-8 mb-3">For Path 3</h3>
      <p class="text-[17px] text-gray-300 font-light leading-relaxed">
        Focus on the wave: inhale 4 seconds, exhale 4 seconds. No emphasis on pausing. Let the rhythm flow like a wave. Practice this daily. The pause will appear on its own when your system is ready.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-16">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-light tracking-tight text-white mb-5">Why the Breathline Practice is one of the Most Powerful Practices</h2>
      <ul class="space-y-4 text-[17px] text-gray-300 font-light leading-relaxed mb-6">
        <li><span class="text-white">It’s immediate.</span> You don’t need perfect conditions. You can access it anywhere.</li>
        <li><span class="text-white">It bypasses mental noise.</span> When you drop into the pause, thoughts quiet automatically.</li>
        <li><span class="text-white">It creates space for perception.</span> In the pause, you can see clearly without reactive patterns distorting.</li>
        <li><span class="text-white">It’s where coherence becomes operational.</span> Not just a concept—a state you can access.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="text-[17px] text-gray-300 font-light leading-relaxed">
        Think of the breathline as a doorway. One side: noise, reactivity, chaos. The other side: clarity, coherence, stillness. Your breath is the key.
      </p>
    </section>

    <GuideVisual kind="doorway" />

    <section class="mb-14">
      <p class="text-[17px] text-gray-300 font-light leading-relaxed">
        All three paths converge at the same place. There’s no hierarchy. Just different entry points based on your nervous system’s current capacity. The breathline is always there, waiting. You’ll access it when your system is ready.
      </p>
    </section>

    <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 text-[13px] mb-12">
      <a href="/breathline/" class="text-signal hover:text-sapphire-400 transition-colors">Start the Breathline exercise →</a>
      <a href="/morning-standing-wave/" class="text-gray-400 hover:text-white transition-colors">Set your morning standing wave →</a>
    </div>

    <GuideDisclaimer />
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
